---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";

// Keep this in step with PROVIDER_CLIENT_DEFINITIONS in
// `apps/web/src/components/settings/providerDriverMeta.ts`.
const providers = [
  ["Claude Code", "/providers/claude.svg", "Anthropic CLI. Enabled by default."],
  ["Codex", "/providers/codex.svg", "OpenAI CLI. Enabled by default."],
  ["Cursor", "/providers/cursor.svg", "Cursor Agent CLI. Enabled by default."],
  ["Grok", "/providers/grok.svg", "Grok CLI. Enabled by default."],
  ["Gemini", "/providers/gemini.svg", "Google Gemini CLI. Enabled by default."],
  ["Meta", "/brand/marks/meta.svg", "Muse Code CLI (muse --acp). Muse Spark. Enabled by default. Badge: New."],
  ["GitHub Copilot", "/brand/marks/copilot.svg", "Copilot CLI (copilot --acp). Opt-in."],
  ["Antigravity", "/providers/antigravity.svg", "Antigravity CLI (agy)"],
  ["Factory Droid", "/providers/droid.svg", "Factory Droid CLI"],
  ["Kilo Code", "/providers/kilo.svg", "Kilo Code CLI"],
  ["OpenCode", "/providers/opencode.svg", "OpenCode with its configured models"],
  ["Pi", "/providers/pi.svg", "Pi coding agent"],
  ["Devin", "/providers/devin.svg", "Devin CLI over ACP. Early access."],
  ["Poolside", "/providers/poolside.svg", "Poolside coding agent. Early access."],
  ["Kimi", "/providers/kimi.svg", "Kimi CLI over ACP. Early access."],
  ["Qwen", "/providers/qwen.svg", "Qwen Code CLI. Early access."],
];

const toc = [
  { label: "How discovery works", href: "#discovery" },
  { label: "Supported providers", href: "#supported" },
  { label: "Login commands", href: "#login" },
  { label: "Authentication", href: "#authentication" },
  { label: "Permissions", href: "#permissions" },
  { label: "Multiple instances", href: "#instances" },
  { label: "Switching providers", href: "#switching" },
  { label: "Provider states", href: "#states" },
];
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Providers"
  description="Connect the coding agents Modesto supports — including Meta Muse Code and GitHub Copilot — and choose the right provider for each turn."
  currentPath="/docs/providers"
  eyebrow="The workspace"
  {toc}
>
  <section id="discovery">
    <h2>How provider discovery works</h2>
    <p>
      Modesto checks for supported local CLIs and reads the provider capabilities
      they expose. Codex, Claude, Cursor, Gemini, Grok, and Meta are enabled by
      default. The rest wait until you turn the instance on. Availability can
      still vary by machine, account, project, and PATH.
    </p>
    <div class="docs-callout"><strong>Keep vendor setup vendor-native.</strong><p>Install, update, and authenticate each CLI through its own supported flow. Modesto uses that existing configuration.</p></div>
  </section>

  <section id="supported">
    <h2>Supported providers</h2>
    <div class="docs-card-grid">
      {providers.map(([name, icon, setup]) => (
        <article class="docs-card">
          <img src={icon} alt="" width="28" height="28" />
          <h3>{name}</h3>
          <p>{setup}</p>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>
    <p>
      You can also add a <strong>Custom ACP Agent</strong> instance that points
      at any CLI speaking the Agent Client Protocol. Use that for drivers
      Modesto does not ship a named adapter for.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="login">
    <h2>Login commands</h2>
    <p>
      Run these on the machine that hosts Modesto, not on a phone you paired
      later. Cursor is the one to watch: the binary is <code>cursor-agent</code>,
      the login command is <code>agent login</code>.
    </p>
    <div class="docs-table">
      <div><strong>Claude Code</strong><span><code>claude auth login</code></span></div>
      <div><strong>Codex</strong><span><code>codex login</code></span></div>
      <div><strong>Cursor</strong><span><code>agent login</code> (binary <code>cursor-agent</code>)</span></div>
      <div><strong>Grok</strong><span><code>grok login</code></span></div>
      <div>
        <strong>Meta (Muse Code)</strong>
        <span>
          Binary <code>muse</code>. Sessions spawn <code>muse --acp</code>. Sign
          in with <code>muse login</code>, or set <code>META_API_KEY</code> /
          <code>MODEL_API_KEY</code>. Default model is Muse Spark 1.3.
        </span>
      </div>
      <div>
        <strong>GitHub Copilot</strong>
        <span>
          Binary <code>copilot</code>. Sessions spawn <code>copilot --acp</code>.
          Enable the instance in settings — it is off by default.
        </span>
      </div>
      <div><strong>OpenCode</strong><span><code>opencode auth login</code></span></div>
      <div><strong>Gemini and others</strong><span>Use that CLI’s native login, then confirm Modesto discovers the binary.</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="docs-code">
      <div class="docs-code-head"><span>Provider check</span><span>Terminal</span></div>
      <pre><code>codex --version
claude --version
gemini --version
cursor-agent --version
muse --version
copilot --version
opencode --version</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="authentication">
    <h2>Authentication</h2>
    <p>
      Sign in through the provider CLI before launching a live session. If a
      provider supports API-key configuration, keep the credential in its
      approved local configuration or Modesto’s permission-protected secret store.
    </p>
    <p>
      If discovery misses a CLI, set <strong>Binary path</strong> on the
      instance. Version managers (nvm, asdf, Homebrew shims) often leave the
      GUI process with a thinner PATH than your terminal.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="permissions">
    <h2>Permissions and execution</h2>
    <p>
      Permissions belong to each run. Review whether the agent can edit files,
      execute commands, use a local checkout, or create an isolated worktree.
      See <a href="/docs/permissions">Permission modes</a> for Supervised,
      Auto-accept edits, Auto, Full access, and Plan. Automations persist a
      permission snapshot so later runs stay predictable.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="instances">
    <h2>Run more than one instance of a provider</h2>
    <p>
      A provider is split into two things: the <strong>driver</strong> that speaks the
      protocol, and an <strong>instance</strong> that routes work to it. Because threads and
      sessions bind to an instance rather than a driver, you can configure the same
      driver more than once — a <code>codex_personal</code> and a <code>codex_work</code>,
      each with its own environment and credentials — and pick the right one per turn.
    </p>
    <div class="docs-table">
      <div><strong>Driver</strong><span>Which implementation handles the protocol, probe, and adapter — <code>codex</code>, <code>claudeAgent</code>, and the rest.</span></div>
      <div><strong>Instance</strong><span>A named routing key you define. Threads, sessions, and persisted bindings reference instance ids.</span></div>
      <div><strong>Instance config</strong><span>Per-instance environment variables and driver-specific settings, kept separate between instances.</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="docs-callout">
      <strong>Unknown drivers degrade instead of crashing.</strong>
      <p>
        Driver names are open, not a fixed list, so settings and thread history that
        reference a provider your current build doesn't ship — from a fork, a PR build,
        or an older branch — still load. The instance is marked unavailable and
        everything else keeps working.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="switching">
    <h2>Switch providers without restarting the task</h2>
    <p>
      Provider choice belongs to a turn, not to the entire project. Create a
      checkpoint before switching so the destination agent receives the
      objective, current Git state, decisions, unresolved work, checks, and next
      action—not only a transcript.
    </p>
    <div class="docs-callout"><strong>Capabilities can differ.</strong><p>A model, tool, permission mode, or resume behavior available through one provider may not exist in another. Review the destination run settings before sending the handoff.</p></div>
  </section>

  <section id="states">
    <h2>Provider states and recovery</h2>
    <div class="docs-table">
      <div><strong>Available</strong><span>The CLI was discovered and can be selected for a run.</span></div>
      <div><strong>Authentication required</strong><span>The executable exists, but its native sign-in or credential setup is incomplete.</span></div>
      <div><strong>Unavailable</strong><span>The CLI is missing, disabled, or cannot start in the current environment.</span></div>
      <div><strong>Disconnected</strong><span>An active provider process ended or lost its session. Resume the thread after correcting the provider-side failure.</span></div>
    </div>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
